ps://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/gabrieldrs/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2019-10-08T21:02:39Z","updated_at":"2019-10-08T21:02:39Z","body":"Hello,\r\n\r\nI took a look at this issue as I encountered it in a project I was working on.\r\nThe problem seems to be that, in order to know if the exception object is inside its exception handler, the rule is comparing the scope of the object (in the example `ex`) with the scope of the exception handler, and, in this case, the scope of `ex` is the generator. If we instead continued to go up in the parent chain, we would find the actual exception handler.\r\n\r\nI imagine we could just check if the object `ex` is inside its exception handler by going up in the parent chain until we find its exception handler, or, if we end up in the root module, we would consider that it's being accessed outside its handler, do you think this approach would be correct?\r\n\r\nI'm new to the project so I may be overlooking things a little, so, please, let me know. I could also put my \"fix\" idea together in a commit and share it with you.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pylint-dev/pylint/issues/comments/539703101/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"gabrieldrs","id":7755088,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc3NTUw